# requirements.in — human-curated source of truth for Python dependencies.
#
# `requirements.txt` is treated as the LOCKFILE (fully pinned, deterministic).
# Regenerate it from this file with pip-tools:
#
#     pip install "pip-tools>=7.4"
#     pip-compile --strip-extras --generate-hashes \
#                 --output-file requirements.txt requirements.in
#
# Always commit both files together.  Dockerfiles install from
# `requirements.txt` so they stay reproducible.
#
# `requirements-py310.txt` is a SECOND lockfile, compiled under Python 3.10
# (`make lock-py310`), used only by the oldest-interpreter leg of the CI test
# matrix.  It exists because the 3.12 lock has no 3.10-only backports
# (exceptiongroup, tomli) and `--require-hashes` will not resolve them on the
# fly.  Nothing ships from it — images and 3.12 keep using requirements.txt.
# Regenerate it whenever you regenerate requirements.txt.
#
# Loose constraints below are the minimum versions we support; the lockfile
# nails the exact build.
PyYAML>=6.0
requests>=2.31.0
PySocks>=1.7.1
